\title{Recurrence for subset sum}
\date{2026-11-06T22:43:36Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\def\cAT{#{\mathit{canAddTo}}}

\p{The key to solving this problem is to generalize it along \em{two} dimensions: we consider both summing to any #{s \leq S}, and we also consider trying to use only the #{x_i} up to #{x_k}, that is, #{\{x_1, \dots, x_k\}}, instead of the full set #{X}. That is, #{\cAT(k,s)} will be \True if there is a subset of #{\{x_1, \dots, x_k\}} which adds to exactly #{s}, and \False otherwise. We have the following recurrence:
##{\begin{align*}
  \cAT(k, 0) &= \True \qquad (\text{for all } k \geq 0) \\
  \cAT(0, s) &= \False \qquad (\text{for all } s > 0) \\
  \cAT(k,s) &=
    \begin{cases}
      \cAT(k-1, s) & \text{if } x_k > s \\
      \cAT(k-1, s) \lor \cAT(k-1, s - x_k) & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}}
}

\p{That is,
\ul{
  \li{We can always add to the sum #{0} by picking the empty subset.}
  \li{We can never add up to a nonzero sum if we aren’t allowed to use any of the #{x_i}.}
  \li{If #{x_k > s}, then it can’t be used in a subset that sums to #{s}, so whether we can make the sum #{s} using a subset of #{x_1 \dots x_k} has the same answer as whether we can make #{s} using a subset of #{x_1 \dots x_{k-1}}.}
  \li{Otherwise, we can try both omitting #{x_k} (in which case we have to make #{s} using elements up to #{x_{k-1}}, as before), or using it (in which case we have to make the remaining #{s - x_k} using the elements up to #{x_{k-1}}).}
}
}

\p{Note that the above is essentially a proof by induction of the
recurrence relation's correctness.  We explained why the base cases
are correct, and then explained why the recursive cases will compute
the right thing, given the assumption that the recursive calls will
return the correct answer when given smaller inputs.}
